  "name": "Alcaraz Flats",
  "region": "southern edge of invented Tamsen County, a Bay Area-style bayside valley",
  "summary": "Alcaraz Flats is a flat, sprawling town of about 80,000 wedged between salt marsh and the foothills, where 1960s ranch houses sit beside new podium apartments and a belt of distribution warehouses. Families here are Mexican, Salvadoran, Filipino, Vietnamese, Punjabi and older white and Black households who bought in before prices tripled. Most adults commute out on the Tule Line train or work locally at the warehouses, the hospital, or the schools. The town argues constantly about whether more warehouses mean jobs or diesel asthma, and whether a downtown housing tower would save Calle Ortega or kill it.",
